{"hash":"407abb5794012f72","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":75.808,"said":"You are now in the mukha mandapa, the pillared hall given in 1405 by King Harihara the Second, husband of the queen Vittalamba whose steps you heard about at the gate. Rest a hand on one of these pillars, and think of who else has passed this way. Adi Shankaracharya, the great teacher, is said to have stayed at Srisailam and composed his Shivanandalahari here, a garland of verses in praise of Shiva. In the twelfth century came the poet-saint Akka Mahadevi. She called Shiva Chennamallikarjuna, ‘the lord white as jasmine’, and by tradition she did penance in the caves of these hills; the temple keeps her image still. Ascetics of many Shaiva orders made their homes in the mathas here, and later the Vijayanagara emperor Krishnadevaraya is credited with the long pillared halls in front of the temple. Kings and poets, wandering sadhus and ordinary families: all of them walked toward the same small doorway that lies just ahead of you. Now it is your turn. Step forward to the sanctum.\n\nDid you know? Verse 50 of the Shivanandalahari ends: ‘seve Shrigiri-Mallikarjuna-mahalingam’, ‘I worship the great linga of Mallikarjuna on Sri Giri’.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":991},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1007,"end":1143}],"words":[[0.098,0.168,0,3],[0.278,0.12,4,7],[0.411,0.337,8,11],[0.759,0.132,12,14],[0.903,0.084,15,18],[1.0,0.337,19,24],[1.348,0.601,25,32],[2.117,0.096,34,37],[2.225,0.349,38,46],[2.586,0.276,47,51],[2.874,0.337,52,57],[3.223,1.31,58,65],[4.545,0.18,66,68],[4.737,0.216,69,73],[4.966,0.373,74,82],[5.351,0.108,83,86],[5.471,0.505,87,93],[6.228,0.433,95,102],[6.673,0.108,103,105],[6.793,0.096,106,109],[6.901,0.3,110,115],[7.213,0.697,116,126],[7.923,0.228,127,132],[8.163,0.385,133,138],[8.56,0.144,139,142],[8.716,0.216,143,148],[8.944,0.397,149,154],[9.353,0.12,155,157],[9.485,0.096,158,161],[9.593,0.397,162,166],[10.877,0.349,168,172],[11.238,0.06,173,